====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[squeeze]] through a [[ricer]]; to [[mash]] or make into rice-[[sized]] pieces (especially {{l|en|potato|potatoes}}).
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Miss Parloa's New Cook Book: A Guide to Marketing and Cooking|year=1881|author=Maria Parloa|passage='''Riced''' Potato. Have a flat dish and the colander hot. With a spoon, rub mashed potato through the colander on to the hot dish.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=bEIbAAAAYAAJ&dq=%22riced%22%20%22potato%22&pg=PA239#v=onepage&q=%22riced%22%20%22potato%22&f=false}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Potato Chipper|volume=21|page=88|year=1961|passage=Following '''ricing''', the potato mash proceeds to the drum drier where flaking is done.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=As Love Blooms (The Gregory Sisters Book #3): A Novel|author=Lorna Seilstad|year=2015|passage=Last night I '''riced''' the potatoes and added in the cream and butter while they were hot, so today wll we have to do is add flour and roll them out.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[harvest]] [[wild rice]] (''[[Zizania]]'' sp.)
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=Botanical Gazette|page=505|author=John Merle Coulter|year=1894|passage=In northern Minnesota the whites have invented the verb "to '''rice'''," and speak of "'''ricing'''," i. e., harvesting the crop of wild rice.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=xqzzAAAAMAAJ&dq=%22ricing%22%20%22potato%22&pg=PA505#v=onepage&q=%22ricing%22%20%22potato%22&f=false}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Wild rice and the Ojibway people|author=Thomas Vennum|year=1988|passage=When '''ricing''', the Ojibway dress warmly at first; by midday they may shed some clothes as harvest toil combines with the hot sun of late summer to warm them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A Capital Place: Reminiscences of a Sandy Lake Boyhood|author=David Laursen|year=2002|passage=As it was, the Indian seldom bothered to harvest wild rice on public waters after opening day of the '''ricing''' season.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-lWtBAAAQBAJ&lpg=PT143&dq=%22ricing%22&pg=PT143#v=onepage&q=%22ricing%22&f=false}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} To throw rice at a person (usually at a wedding).
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=The Illustrated London News|volume=89|page=90|date=July 24 1886|passage=So far as I can make out, the idiotic function of “'''ricing'''” English brides and bridegrooms is not twenty years old.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=R4o-AQAAMAAJ&dq=%22ricing%22%20%22wedding%22&pg=PA90#v=onepage&q=%22ricing%22%20%22wedding%22&f=false|title=Echoes of the Week}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Gilded Lily|author=Helen Argers|year=2002|passage=The couple was well '''riced''' and sent on their way.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ZGqqwgJuSi0C&lpg=PA300&dq=%22riced%22%20%22wedding%22&pg=PA300#v=onepage&q=%22riced%22%20%22wedding%22&f=false}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Billy: A Gay Trilogy|year=2006|author=Timothy Lee|passage=As the reception ended the two newlyweds were '''riced''' to death and fled into an awaiting getaway car and drove off...followed by a stream of tin cans.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|transitive}} To [[customize]] the [[user interface]] of a [[computer]] system, e.g. a [[desktop environment]].

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|ris}}, {{m|enm|rys}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hrīs||branch; twig}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*hrīsą||bush; twig}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*(s)kreys-||to turn; bend; wind; move; shake}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|reise}}, {{m|sco|rice||twigs; brushwood}}, {{cog|fy|riis}}, {{m|fy|rys}}, {{cog|nl|rijs||little branch; twig; osier; whip}}, {{cog|nds-de|Ries}}, {{cog|de|Reis||twig; sprig; shoot}}, {{cog|sv|ris||twigs; brush; rod}}, {{cog|is|hrís}}.

