# {{lb|en|now chiefly|dialectal|Scotland|Ireland}} A {{l|en|twig}} or {{l|en|stick}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A systematic treatise on the theory and practice of draining land|author=John Johnstone|year=1834|passage=To guard the bank from the impression of the water, a fence, OF STAKE AND '''RICE''', may be made along the bottom of it next the sea, which will last till the surface on that side is sufficiently swarded, and the mound properly consolidated.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=m7FfAAAAcAAJ&dq=%22rice%22%20%22fence%22&pg=PA168#v=onepage&q=%22rice%22%20%22fence%22&f=false}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Book of the Farm|volume=1|author=Henry Stephens|passage=Another form of dead-hedge is the ''stake-and-'''rice''''', and it is formed of the branches of forest trees; and where these are plentiful and thorns scarce, it is an economical dead fence.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=QPaJmMSYiFMC&dq=%22fence%22%20%22rice%22&pg=PA579#v=onepage&q=%22fence%22%20%22rice%22&f=false|year=1851}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=Notes and Queries|series=6|volume=6|date=July 16 1881|passage="Gilbert White, the well-known naturalist, in a letter dated Selborne, Oct. 4th, 1775, says, 'Our people here, you know, call coppice-wood or hedge-wood '''''rice''''' or '''''rise'''''. Is this word still in use in that neighbourhood? And is it also known in Surrey?"|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=MuU1AQAAMAAJ&dq=%22hedge%22%20%22ryce%22&pg=PA53#v=onepage&q=%22hedge%22%20%22ryce%22&f=false}}
# {{lb|en|weaving|obsolete}} A {{l|en|bobbin}} or {{l|en|spool}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Middlesex County Records|year=1892|passage={{...}} taken unlawfully from the same house five "machines called 'Engine-Weaving Loomes' worth thirty pounds, and two ounces of silke worth five shillings, and two joynt-stooles worth three shillings, and a pair of ''''Rices''' to wind silke on' worth four shillings {{...}}|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=AI4NAAAAIAAJ&dq=%22rices%22%20%22weaving%22&pg=PR29#v=onepage&q=%22rices%22%20%22weaving%22&f=false|editor=John Cordy Jeaffreson|volume=4}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Cotton Weaving: Its Development, Principles, and Practice|author=Richard Marsden|year=1895|passage=The hanks are placed upon light, collapsible hexagon reels termed '''rices''', which are easily lifted out of their position for the reception of the hank.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wLJLAAAAIAAJ&dq=%22rices%22%20%22weaving%22&pg=PA272#v=onepage&q=%22rices%22%20%22weaving%22&f=false}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Hand weaving and cloth design|author=Marianne Straub|year=1977|passage=Swift ('''rice''') Skein holder, hank holder.}}

